## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of persistent data structures and O(1) updates without copying.

## Key Concepts
- Persistence via structural sharing
- Node-based linked representation
- Versioning by top pointer
- Space-time trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node { constructor(val, prev){ this.val = val; this.prev = prev; } }
class PersistentStack {
  constructor() { this.versions = [null]; } // version 0: empty
  push(version, val) { const top = this.versions[version]; const node = new Node(val, top); this.versions.push(node); return this.versions.length - 1; }
  pop(version) { const top = this.versions[version]; if (!top) return version; this.versions.push(top.prev); return this.versions.length - 1; }
  top(version) { const t = this.versions[version]; return t ? t.val : null; }
  isEmpty(version) { return this.versions[version] == null; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you manage memory for old versions (garbage collection)?
- How would you expose the API to external callers (version IDs, immutability guarantees)?
- Could you adapt this to a persistent queue or other structures with similar patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of advanced data structure design by combining segment trees with multisets to handle weighted intervals with duplicate support. It also evaluates understanding of binary search applied to aggregated data structures for kth largest queries.

## Key Concepts
- **Coordinate compression** on endpoints to discretize the domain
- **Segment tree** where each node stores a multiset of weights for intervals covering that node's range
- **Interval decomposition** into O(log n) nodes for efficient updates
- **Path aggregation** for point sum queries
- **Binary search over weight values** with counts from multisets to support kthLargest queries

## Code Example
```javascript
class IntervalDS {
  constructor(coords) {
    this.coords = coords;
    this.N = coords.length;
    this.tree = Array(4 * this.N).fill(null).map(() => new Map());
    this.sums = Array(4 * this.N).fill(0);
  }

  insert(l, r, w) {
    this.update(1, 0, this.N - 1, l, r, w, 1);
  }

  erase(l, r, w) {
    this.update(1, 0, this.N - 1, l, r, w, -1);
  }

  update(node, start, end, l, r, w, delta) {
    if (r < start || end < l) return;
    if (l <= start && end <= r) {
      const count = this.tree[node].get(w) || 0;
      this.tree[node].set(w, count + delta);
      if (this.tree[node].get(w) === 0) this.tree[node].delete(w);
      this.sums[node] += w * delta;
      return;
    }
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    this.update(node * 2, start, mid, l, r, w, delta);
    this.update(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, l, r, w, delta);
    this.sums[node] = this.sums[node * 2] + this.sums[node * 2 + 1];
  }

  sumAtPoint(x) {
    return this.querySum(1, 0, this.N - 1, x);
  }

  querySum(node, start, end, x) {
    if (x < start || x > end) return 0;
    if (start === end) return this.sums[node];
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    return this.sums[node] + 
      (x <= mid ? this.querySum(node * 2, start, mid, x) 
                : this.querySum(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, x));
  }

  kthLargest(x, k) {
    const weights = [...new Set(this.getWeightsAtPoint(x))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
    if (k > weights.length) return null;
    return weights[k - 1];
  }

  getWeightsAtPoint(x) {
    const result = [];
    this.collectWeights(1, 0, this.N - 1, x, result);
    return result;
  }

  collectWeights(node, start, end, x, result) {
    if (x < start || x > end) return;
    for (const [w, count] of this.tree[node]) {
      result.push(...Array(count).fill(w));
    }
    if (start === end) return;
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    this.collectWeights(node * 2, start, mid, x, result);
    this.collectWeights(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, x, result);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an order-statistics structure with a global offset that shifts all values, while correctly handling duplicates and mapping erasures to the current offset.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics BST (treap/AVL with size augmentation)
- Global lazy offset for all elements
- Handling duplicates via per-key counts
- Correct mapping for erase when offset changes

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(key, cnt = 1) {
    this.key = key; // stored key = actual_value - offset
    this.cnt = cnt;
    this.pri = Math.random();
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
    this.sz = cnt;
  }
}
function sz(n){ return n ? n.sz : 0; }
function upd(n){ if(n) n.sz = n.cnt + sz(n.left) + sz(n.right); }
function rotateRight(p){ const q = p.left; p.left = q.right; q.right = p; upd(p); upd(q); return q; }
function rotateLeft(p){ const q = p.right; p.right = q.left; q.left = p; upd(p); upd(q); return q; }
// Insert, erase, kthSmallest would follow standard treap logic using stored keys (x - offset)
```

